VineBot – Habitat simulation with ML obstacle avoidance
=======================================================

Before you start (once)
# - the workspace is at ~/robo_project_ws (otherwise change the "cd" lines below)
#   NOTE 2026-11-12: on this PC it is currently one level deeper, ~/robo_project_ws/robo_project_ws. Either
#   use that path in the cd lines, or move it up once (no terminals running), then rebuild:
#     mv ~/robo_project_ws/robo_project_ws ~/rpw_tmp && rmdir ~/robo_project_ws && mv ~/rpw_tmp ~/robo_project_ws
#     cd ~/robo_project_ws && rm -rf build install && colcon build --cmake-args -DPython3_EXECUTABLE=/usr/bin/python3
# - conda env "habitat" with habitat_sim, torch and numba; ROS 2 Humble in /opt/ros/humble
# - the ML checkpoint at ~/robo_project_ws/runs/fusion_mid360_e3.0/best.pt (not in git, 72 MB)
# - the scene src/environment/IMR_lab8.glb + .navmesh and the map src/resources/sketch_map_imr_lab8.jpeg
# Simulation: the terminals below. Real robot (motors + PS4): section "REAL ROBOT" near the end.

Run order: build once -> Habitat -> robot model -> RViz -> runner (map) -> ML model -> planner.
Wait for each step's "ready" line before starting the next one. Stop everything with Ctrl+C.

terminal 1  (build once - do NOT rebuild while the other terminals are running)

conda activate habitat
cd ~/robo_project_ws
rm -rf build install log
colcon build --packages-select robo_project vinebot_description --cmake-args -DPython3_EXECUTABLE=/usr/bin/python3
source install/setup.bash

terminal 2  (Habitat simulation - sim/habitat_bridge.py)

conda activate habitat
cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
python3 -m robo_project.sim.habitat_bridge
# ready when: "Robot spawned at x=-4.10 y=4.90" and "Habitat Bridge fully initialized"
# publishes the 4 cameras, front depth, the simulated LiDAR (/livox/lidar), /odom and TF.
# LiDAR: realistic Mid-360 at the front mount (beam pattern measured on the real robot: it does not
#   see low obstacles close to the robot). -p lidar_model:=planar = old flat scan that sees everything.
# drive: -p drive_model:=tracked  = the real rubber tracks (skid steering, 140 kg): speed changes take time
#   (track_lin_accel 0.5 m/s^2, track_ang_accel 1.5 rad/s^2) and turns slip (track_turn_efficiency 0.75 =
#   the robot turns 75% of the commanded rate). Estimates - measure them on the robot. Default "ideal".
# options:  --ros-args -p start_x:=-4.1 -p start_y:=4.9 -p start_yaw:=0.0
#           -p gpu_device_id:=-1    (no NVIDIA GPU / VM: software rendering)
#           -p show_window:=false   (no camera window)

terminal 3  (robot model)

cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
ros2 launch vinebot_description robot_launch.py

terminal 4  (RViz)

conda activate habitat
cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
rviz2 -d src/vinebot_description/config/display_vinebot.rviz
# shows: map, robot, planned path, goal, current waypoint, "ML Obstacle Map" (what the planner
# avoids), "Fusion Local Grid" (raw 3 m model prediction), "LiDAR" (simulated scan),
# "Sensor Rig (measured)" (needs the rig display below)
# 3D view following the robot (to see the camera heights): use display_vinebot_3d.rviz instead
#
# rig display (optional, another terminal): footprint, cameras and LiDAR at their measured heights
#   python3 src/robo_project/sim/tools/rig_viz.py

terminal 5  (runner: publishes /map from the sketch + follows /planned_path - ros/runner_node.py)

conda activate habitat
cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
ros2 run robo_project runner_node
# ready when: "MAP BROADCAST SUCCESSFUL! Check RViz now."
# map: src/resources/sketch_map_imr_lab8.jpeg

terminal 6  (ML model: 4 RGB cameras + LiDAR + depth -> obstacle map - ml/fusion/fusion_obstacle_node.py)

conda activate habitat
cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
ros2 run robo_project fusion_obstacle_node
# ready when: "Obstacle map 768x1024 ... aligned with /map"
# every prediction (~10/s, ~85 ms each on CPU) is fused into /fusion/obstacle_map.
# model: runs/fusion_mid360_e3.0/best.pt (measured rig in config/vinebot_rig.yaml incl. the realistic
#   Livox Mid-360 at the front, 3 m grid). Falls back to runs/fusion_real_rig_e3.0, then fusion_full_e1.2.
# Besides the model's 3 m grid, the raw LiDAR is fused into the map out to 4 m
#   (-p lidar_map_range:=4.0, -p lidar_map:=false to switch off). LiDAR free space only where its lowest
#   beam could see a 25 cm obstacle (-p lidar_free_max_height:=0.25).
# Real robot: /livox/lidar in the sensor frame (livox_frame) is moved to base_footprint with the rig
#   LiDAR mounting (x 0.395, height 0.45, yaw 90) and the robot's own returns are removed.

terminal 7  (A* planner - planning/motion_planner.py)

conda activate habitat
cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
python3 -m robo_project.planning.motion_planner
# run it with the conda python (NOT "ros2 run": that uses /usr/bin/python3, which has no numba,
# and A* then falls back to the slow pure-Python search: seconds instead of ~10 ms).
# plans to (0,0) at start. Click "2D Goal Pose" in RViz for a new goal.
# Obstacles from the ML model are added to the A* map; when one lies on the current path it
# re-plans at once ("ML model: obstacle on the path"). No path at all: the robot is stopped and
# planning is retried every 2 s. If the robot still gets stuck, runner_node reports it on
# /blocked_point and the planner re-plans around it.

terminal 8  (optional: record a run, while terminals 2-7 are running; Ctrl+C to stop recording)

cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
mkdir -p bags
ros2 bag record -o bags/my_run /odom /tf /tf_static /cmd_vel /map /planned_path /blocked_point /goal_pose /fusion/local_grid /fusion/obstacle_map /livox/lidar /camera/front/depth/image_raw
# ~4 MB/s. Add /camera/{front,right,back,left}/image_raw to also keep the RGB images (~6 MB/s more).

terminal 9  (optional: drive a fixed goal sequence instead of clicking goals in RViz)

conda activate habitat
cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
python3 src/robo_project/sim/tools/goal_sequencer.py

after recording  (analyse the run; results go to bags/my_run/analysis)

conda activate habitat
cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
python3 src/robo_project/sim/tools/analyze_collisions.py bags/my_run src/environment/IMR_lab8.navmesh bags/my_run/analysis
python3 src/robo_project/ml/tools/eval_model.py bags/my_run src/environment/IMR_lab8.navmesh src/robo_project bags/my_run/analysis
# analyze_collisions: contacts in Habitat + robot body overlapping obstacles, with a map image
# eval_model: ML model accuracy vs ground truth, compared with LiDAR alone

LOCALIZATION  (sketch particle filter - localization/sketch_pf_node.py)

# The filter tracks the pose on the hand-drawn /map with odometry + the ML grid (/fusion/local_grid) and
# publishes /localization/odom. Runner, planner and ML node then use it INSTEAD of the true /odom by a
# remap only:  --ros-args -r /odom:=/localization/odom   (no code change in those nodes)
# Start pose: -p init_x/init_y/init_yaw (default = simulation spawn -4.10 4.90 0) or RViz "2D Pose Estimate".
# Accuracy (simulation): 4 cm on a perfect map; ~0.7 m mean (max ~1.2 m) on the hand-drawn sketch, because
#   the sketch itself is drawn up to ~0.8 m off reality (on the real robot the sketch IS the map).
# Defaults = wheels + IMU odometry (EKF). Wheel odometry ONLY: add
#   -p noise_trans_per_m:=0.08 -p noise_rot_per_rad:=0.10 -p corr_rot_per_rad:=0.15 -p corr_rot_per_m:=0.05
#   (tracks slip in turns -> heading errors up to ~25 deg -> an IMU is needed for safe autonomous driving)
# RViz: "Localization Particles" (green arrows) and "Localization Pose" (yellow, with uncertainty).

simulation with localization: terminals 2-4 as above, then instead of terminals 5-7:

terminal L1  (simulated odometry - the real robot does not need this)
conda activate habitat
cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
python3 src/robo_project/sim/tools/wheel_odom_sim.py --ros-args -p rot_scale:=1.01 -p rot_noise:=0.01
# publishes /wheel_odom like wheels + IMU (1% turn error). Without the -p: wheels only (3% distance,
#   8% turn error) - then start the filter with the wheel-only settings above.

terminal L2  (particle filter)
conda activate habitat
cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
ros2 run robo_project sketch_pf_node
# ready when: "Map 768x1024 ..." and "PF started at (-4.10, 4.90, 0 deg)"; logs every 20 updates
# other start: -p init_x:=... -p init_y:=... -p init_yaw:=...   (or "2D Pose Estimate" in RViz)

terminals L3-L5  (runner, ML, planner on the localized pose - start in this order, as terminals 5-7)
ros2 run robo_project runner_node --ros-args -r /odom:=/localization/odom
ros2 run robo_project fusion_obstacle_node --ros-args -r /odom:=/localization/odom -p forget_s:=20.0
python3 -m robo_project.planning.motion_planner --ros-args -r /odom:=/localization/odom
# forget_s: old obstacle evidence fades (pose corrections would otherwise leave ghost obstacles that close
#   corridors). The planner parks with 0.80 m of room from obstacles (robot corner radius 0.65 m).

# whole test headless:  src/robo_project/sim/tools/run_regression.sh results/my_loc_test "" loc_imu
#   (loc = wheel odometry only; loc_imu = wheels + IMU) - prints goals / contacts / overlap and the
#   localization error vs the true pose (sim/tools/loc_error.py)
# tune offline on a recording (seconds per try, also with seed=N to check robustness):
#   python3 src/robo_project/sim/tools/pf_replay.py results/my_test/bag rot_scale=1.01 rot_noise=0.01
# Your friend's first version (live_global_pose_node, localization_navigation_bridge, localization_motion_gate,
#   cmn_*.launch.py) is kept unchanged for reference; it searches the whole map every 10 s at 4 headings
#   and was 1.8-9.3 m off - use sketch_pf_node instead.

regression test  (whole stack headless: 6 goals + the former freeze spot, recorded and analysed, ~10 min)

conda activate habitat
cd ~/robo_project_ws
src/robo_project/sim/tools/run_regression.sh results/my_test
src/robo_project/sim/tools/run_regression.sh results/my_test_tracked "-p drive_model:=tracked"
# stop the other terminals first (the script kills leftover nodes). Prints per goal REACHED/TIMEOUT,
# stalls / NO PATH / contacts, body overlap (analyze_collisions) and model accuracy (eval_model).
# Goals closer than 0.70 m to an obstacle are moved up to 0.5 m by the planner so the 1.15 x 0.60 m body
# fits ("Driving to ... instead"); RViz "Goal" shows where the robot will really park.

check a real LiDAR recording  (LVX2 file from Livox Viewer 2, e.g. ~/Downloads/lidar_data)

conda activate habitat
cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
python3 src/robo_project/ml/tools/real_lidar_check.py ~/Downloads/lidar_data results/real_lidar
# runs the points through what fusion_obstacle_node does on the real robot (rig mounting -> base_footprint,
# self-returns removed, obstacle band, 3 m model grid). results/real_lidar/report.txt: floor height
# (checks the LiDAR height), tilt, lowest visible obstacle per distance; frames.png: pictures.

retrain the ML model  (after the sensor rig changes - config/vinebot_rig.yaml; ml/fusion/)

# once: a separate conda env with GPU torch. PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1 + empty PYTHONPATH are needed because a
# CPU-only torch in ~/.local and the ROS python paths would otherwise shadow the env's packages.
conda create -y -n fusion_train python=3.10
conda activate fusion_train
export PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1 PYTHONPATH=
pip install torch torchvision --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu126
pip install opencv-python-headless numpy pyyaml

# 1. collect samples in Habitat with the rig (conda env habitat, ~38 samples/s, ~42 KB each)
#    LiDAR = realistic Mid-360 at the front mount (sim/lidar_sim.py, beam pattern from the real recording)
conda activate habitat
cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
python3 -m robo_project.ml.fusion.collect_data --scenes src/environment/IMR_lab8.glb src/environment/Denmark.glb --samples-per-scene 20000 --out data/fusion_mid360

# 2. train on the GPU (fine-tune from the previous model; ~80 samples/s on the RTX 3050)
conda activate fusion_train
cd ~/robo_project_ws
export PYTHONNOUSERSITE=1 PYTHONPATH=$HOME/robo_project_ws/src
python -m robo_project.ml.fusion.train_fusion --data data/fusion_mid360 --extent 3.0 --rig src/robo_project/config/vinebot_rig.yaml --init-from runs/fusion_real_rig_e3.0/best.pt --no-imagenet --epochs 6 --batch 32 --workers 10 --out runs/fusion_mid360_e3.0
# writes best.pt, report.json (validation scores incl. LiDAR-only baseline) and examples.png.
# fusion_obstacle_node picks runs/fusion_mid360_e3.0/best.pt automatically (else fusion_real_rig_e3.0, fusion_full_e1.2),
# and warns when the model was trained for another rig than config/vinebot_rig.yaml.

REAL ROBOT  (motors + PS4 joystick - packages serial, ps4_ros2, roboteq_controller_ros2, vinebot_bringup)

# once: system packages, then build (system python, no conda needed)
sudo apt install ros-humble-ros2-control ros-humble-ros2-controllers ros-humble-diagnostic-updater ros-humble-twist-mux
cd ~/robo_project_ws
source /opt/ros/humble/setup.bash
colcon build --packages-select serial roboteq ps4_ros2 vinebot_bringup
# whole workspace at once (7 packages): colcon build --cmake-args -DPython3_EXECUTABLE=/usr/bin/python3

robot terminal 1  (motors, joystick, who-drives logic, robot model for TF)

cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
ros2 launch vinebot_bringup real_robot.launch.py
# ready when: "Configured and activated roboteq_controller" and "... joint_state_broadcaster"
# options: serial_port:=/dev/ttyUSB0  baud_rate:=115200  joystick:=false  enable_button:=4 (L1)
#          confirm_moves:=true (default; autonomy only via robot terminal 1b)
#          use_mock_hardware:=true  (no robot attached: test the whole chain on a laptop)
# before driving: release the ORANGE e-stop remote (unscrew the black button, press green) and keep it
#   in your hand - it is the real emergency stop.
# PS4: calibrate first (follow the ps4_ros2 prints). Drive only while holding L1:
#   R2/L2 = forward/back (1/10 speed; with Square 1/5, with X full), left stick = turn.
#   O = stop everything (/emergency lock in twist_mux). Release L1 -> one stop, then autonomy may drive.
# who drives (twist_mux, vinebot_bringup/config/twist_mux.yaml):
#   joystick /cmd_vel_joy (only while L1 held) > autonomy /cmd_vel (our runner_node) ; /emergency blocks all
#   -> /roboteq_controller/cmd_vel_unstamped. Odometry: /odom + TF odom -> base_footprint (wheel encoders).
# robot model for TF = our vinebot_description (base_footprint at the track centre, sensors as in
#   config/vinebot_rig.yaml); the roboteq model is only used inside the controller_manager.
# check:  ros2 control list_controllers   (both "active")

robot terminal 1b  (move confirmation - run it in its OWN terminal, it asks here)

cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
ros2 run vinebot_bringup motion_confirm
# with confirm_moves:=true (default of real_robot.launch.py) the autonomy (/cmd_vel of runner_node)
# reaches the motors ONLY through this node. For every new goal it prints goal, park position,
# path length, waypoints and asks "Move? [y/N]":  y = drive,  anything else = stay,  s = stop any time.
# Re-plans to the same goal do not ask again. Without this terminal the robot never drives autonomously.
# answer from another terminal instead:  ros2 topic pub --once /motion_confirm std_msgs/msg/Bool "{data: true}"
# switch it off: ros2 launch vinebot_bringup real_robot.launch.py confirm_moves:=false
# test it in the SIMULATION: start the bridge with  --ros-args -r /cmd_vel:=/cmd_vel_confirmed

robot terminals 2-4  sensors (drivers not in this workspace - install them once on the robot PC with
  bash ~/robo_project_ws/install_robot_drivers.sh     (asks for the sudo password; Livox is built in ~/drivers_ws)
  then: echo 'source ~/drivers_ws/install/setup.bash' >> ~/.bashrc )
# Livox network: PC wired IPv4 manual 192.168.1.5/24 (default MID360_config.json); Mid-360 = 192.168.1.1XX
#   (XX = last 2 serial digits; default config: .12). Other IP: edit "ip" in
#   ~/drivers_ws/src/livox_ros_driver2/config/MID360_config.json, then (conda OFF, system python):
#   cd ~/drivers_ws && colcon build --packages-select livox_ros_driver2 --cmake-args -DROS_EDITION=ROS2 -DDISTRO_ROS=humble -DPython3_EXECUTABLE=/usr/bin/python3
ros2 launch livox_ros_driver2 rviz_MID360_launch.py                       # /livox/lidar PointCloud2
ros2 launch realsense2_camera rs_launch.py enable_color:=true enable_depth:=true align_depth.enable:=true
#   -> /camera/camera/color/image_raw + /camera/camera/aligned_depth_to_color/image_raw (16UC1 mm: supported)
# side / back cameras -> /camera/left/image_raw, /camera/right/image_raw, /camera/back/image_raw (or pass
#   other names to autonomy.launch.py). IMU (BNO055 or D455 IMU + imu_filter_madgwick) -> /imu/data
#   (only the gyro z rate is used; the robot must stand still for the first 2 s: gyro bias)

robot terminal 5  (AUTONOMY: localization + ML + planner + path follower)
# odometry = vinebot_bringup wheel_imu_odom (started by autonomy.launch.py): distance from the wheels, turn from
#   the IMU gyro, gyro bias learned whenever the robot stands still (leave it still for 2 s after start).
#   odometry:=ekf uses robot_localization instead (installed; 15 deg vs 4 deg mean heading error in the test,
#   because the EKF cannot remove the gyro bias)
cd ~/robo_project_ws
source install/setup.bash
ros2 launch vinebot_bringup real_robot.launch.py odom_topic:=/wheel_odom     # (= robot terminal 1, with this option)
ros2 launch vinebot_bringup autonomy.launch.py init_x:=<x> init_y:=<y> init_yaw:=<rad> python:=$(which python3)
# start pose = where the robot stands on the sketch map (or "2D Pose Estimate" in RViz afterwards).
# python: an interpreter with numba (fast A*), e.g. the conda env. use_ekf:=false = wheels only (worse).
# odometry:=builtin (default) | ekf | auto (ekf if installed)
# imu_yaw_sign:=-1.0 if the IMU is mounted upside down (check: "ros2 topic echo /imu/data" - turning LEFT
#   with the joystick must give a POSITIVE angular_velocity.z). imu_topic:=... if the driver uses another name.
# path follower (runner_node): corners up to 75 deg are driven (40-75 deg as a slow tight curve, if the live ML
#   grid shows room), sharper ones turned on the spot. Before turning
#   on the spot it checks the area the body sweeps on the live ML grid; if blocked it turns the other way
#   round or does a multi-point turn ("manoeuvre: ..." in the log). Obstacle within 12 cm of the front
#   bumper -> it stops and the planner re-plans.
# Moves only after "y" in robot terminal 1b (motion_confirm), joystick (L1) always overrides, O = stop.
# Same launch tested end to end in the simulation: src/robo_project/sim/tools/run_autonomy_test.sh results/x

notes for the real robot
# - Livox: use rviz_MID360_launch.py (PointCloud2); msg_MID360_launch.py publishes Livox CustomMsg, which
#   fusion_obstacle_node cannot read. Keep the extrinsic in MID360_config.json at 0: our rig rotates the
#   points (lidar yaw_deg 90). Check with ml/tools/real_lidar_check.py on a recording ("yaw OK").
# - still to check ON the robot (cannot be tested in simulation): motor directions (drive slowly with the
#   joystick first), the IMU topic name, side/back camera topics and fields of view, and the start pose.
#   First autonomous runs: motion_confirm on, low speed, orange e-stop in hand.
# - to simulate the real controller limits: bridge -p drive_model:=tracked -p track_lin_accel:=0.5
#   -p track_ang_accel:=0.5  (roboteq control.yaml: max 1.0 m/s, 1.0 rad/s, accel 0.5)

